+
+### Access log
+
+Without a collector, the forward proxy also writes an access log to stdout on
+the `seekrit_access` target, at `info`:
+
+- `event="connect"`: each `CONNECT` decision, with `host`, `port`, `action`
+  (`intercept`, `tunnel`, or `deny`), and `reason` (`rule`, `tunnel_rule`,
+  `unmatched_tunnel`, `no_rule`, `port_not_allowed`, `egress_blocked`).
+- `event="request"`: each request the forward plane answered, with `plane`
+  (`http` or `intercept`), `method`, `host`, `port`, `path` (without the query
+  string), `status`, `duration_ms` (until the response started), and `injected`
+  (comma-separated secret **names**).
+- `event="tunnel_closed"`: each blind tunnel, with `bytes_up`, `bytes_down`, and
+  `duration_ms`, or `error`.
+
+Set `SEEKRIT_LOG_FORMAT=json` for one JSON object per line, with an event's
+fields at the top level beside `timestamp`, `level`, and `target`:
+
+```sh
+SEEKRIT_LOG_FORMAT=json RUST_LOG=warn,seekrit_access=info seekrit-proxy
+```
+
+```json
+{"timestamp":"…","level":"INFO","message":"connect","event":"connect","host":"pypi.org","port":443,"action":"tunnel","reason":"tunnel_rule","target":"seekrit_access"}
+```
+
+Paths are logged as the workload sent them, before substitution, so they can
+hold a placeholder's name but never a value. They can still carry identifiers;
+filter the target out if that matters more than the record.
